Now write game-sim.js. Need to be careful with all instanceof-free identities. Boss in `this.boss`; damageEnemy handles both normal enemies and boss via fields.

Boss has no `type` (art drawBoss separate). Some generic logic needs `e.isBoss`.

Write sim in one go maybe ~900 lines, might exceed output limit. Split sim into two files? Could do `game-sim.js` (player + combat) and `game-ai.js` (enemies + boss) and merge methods. Let's plan:

- game-sim.js: update, player, attacks, damage, particles helpers, projectiles, effects, spawn, camera, aim.
- game-ai.js: updateEnemies, updateEnemy, updateBoss, spawnBoss, boss logic, killEnemy? killEnemy uses damage logic; could be in sim but boss death needs. Put killEnemy in ai or sim; either.
- Merge in game.js: `Object.assign(Game.prototype, simMethods, aiMethods, renderMethods)`.

Do that to keep writes manageable.

game-sim.js imports core, data, constants, art? It doesn't draw. It needs rgba? maybe not. Use colors from data. No art needed except colors.

Let's write game-sim.js part 1.

Details to not forget:
- `this.victoryEmitted` reset.
- `this.decals = []` reset in startChamber; declare in constructor? Add via game.js? We can initialize lazily `this.decals ||= []`. Render uses. Add to constructor later perhaps.
- `this.introT`.
- `this.slowmo` reset on startRun/startChamber.
- `this.bossSpawned`.

Attack cooldown values.

Precise methods:

```js
update(rawDt) {
 const input=this.input;
 this.time += rawDt;
 this.shakeAmount = damp(this.shakeAmount, 0, 9, rawDt);
